sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform--claude-opus-5-5--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "In silico trial: aligned versus uniform lattices with simulated print scatter" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform> ;
    dct:description "Run the experiment computationally with the variability the physical test would face. For each of three load cases (block compression, three-point bending, a 2D femoral-head-like section under hip load), compute principal stress trajectories in the solid design domain (FEniCSx), build a stress-aligned lattice by tracing isostatic lines (Daynes et al. 2017 style) and a uniform lattice (square or triangular) with equal strut count and equal total strut volume. Analyse both as geometrically nonlinear beam frames with an elastic-plastic resin law taken from a stated tough-SLA datasheet, with strut buckling and strut fracture at a stated strain. Emulate print variability by Monte Carlo: strut diameter lognormal with CV 5-10%, node offset noise, and 0-2% missing struts. Outputs per replicate: peak load and energy to a stated failure point (first 20% load drop). Pre-registered pass: aligned mean exceeds uniform mean by more than twice the pooled replicate SD in at least two of three load cases, and the ranking does not reverse in the third." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Per load case, the standardized gain (aligned minus uniform, over replicate SD) with CIs, and the print-variability CV at which the gain falls below 2 SD." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: Gains that disappear when the strut-count constraint is enforced (alignment advantage was a strut-density artefact), or results dominated by the assumed failure law." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/simulation" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Pre-register load cases, lattice generation rules, material law, failure definition, variability model and pass criterion. 2. Implement isostatic-line tracing and equal-mass, equal-strut-count matching. 3. Run 200 Monte Carlo replicates per design and load case. 4. Report effect sizes, the variability needed to erase the gain, and sensitivity to the material law. 5. Export the designs as STL for approach 2." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Missing: A simulation is not the printed test the problem asks for; it predicts the effect and the variability that would erase it." ;
    skos:note "Tool: fenicsx" ;
    skos:note "Tool: python" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-05> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-06> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.matdes.2017.04.082> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1089/3dp.2017.0001> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1007/s00158-016-1420-7> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1016/S1359-6454(00)00379-7> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/printed-stress-aligned-lattice-beats-uniform> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does a stress-aligned lattice beat a uniform lattice at equal mass, when printed?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.matdes.2017.04.082> dct:title "Optimisation of functionally graded lattice structures using isostatic lines" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Daynes, Feih, Lu and Wei 2017, Optimisation of functionally graded lattice structures using isostatic lines" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1089/3dp.2017.0001> dct:title "Additive Manufacturing Along Principal Stress Lines" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Tam and Mueller 2017, Additive manufacturing along principal stress lines" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1007/s00158-016-1420-7> dct:title "On the (non-)optimality of Michell structures" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Sigmund, Aage and Andreassen 2016, On the (non-)optimality of Michell structures" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1016/S1359-6454(00)00379-7> dct:title "Foam topology: bending versus stretching dominated architectures" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Deshpande, Ashby and Fleck 2001, Foam topology: bending versus stretching dominated architectures" .
}

